# RUN: llc -mtriple=arceb -mcpu=arc700eb -start-before=arc-delay-slot-filler \
# RUN:     -verify-machineinstrs -o - %s | FileCheck %s

# The AsmPrinter must emit BOTH members of a delay-slot bundle.
#
# A filled slot is a bundle: [delayed transfer, slot instruction]. AsmPrinter
# walks the block with a MachineBasicBlock::iterator, which is a
# bundle_iterator and therefore yields only bundle heads. Without an explicit
# walk in ARCAsmPrinter::emitInstruction the slot instruction is never lowered
# and never reaches the streamer -- it silently disappears from the object
# file, and the delayed transfer then takes whatever word happens to follow it
# as its delay slot and executes that instead.
#
# The walk must be driven off the bundle *flags* (isInsideBundle), not off
# isBundle(): ARCDelaySlotFiller builds its bundles with MIBundleBuilder,
# which only sets BundledSucc/BundledPred and inserts no TargetOpcode::BUNDLE
# header. There is thus nothing for isBundle() to match, and the transfer
# itself is the head -- an isBundle()-keyed walk (the Hexagon idiom, which
# does have real BUNDLE headers) never fires and drops the slot on the floor.
# That exact bug shipped in the first draft of this pass and this test is what
# caught it.
#
# This is a silent wrong-code bug, not a crash: -verify-machineinstrs stays
# quiet and llc exits 0. The only symptom is a short .text -- which is exactly
# what this test catches, by requiring the slot instruction to appear in the
# output immediately after the transfer that owns it.
#
# `-start-before` also means ARCSizeReduction does not run, so the candidate
# stays a 4-byte `add` rather than being shrunk to a 2-byte compact form (only
# 4-byte slot occupants are allowed for now -- see the size guard in
# ARCDelaySlotFiller::delayHasHazard).
#
# Expected: j_s [blink] (0x7EE0) becomes its delayed twin j_s.d [blink]
# (0x7FE0) -- also 2 bytes, so the conversion is size-neutral -- with the
# `add` retired for free in its slot.

# CHECK-LABEL: f:
# CHECK:       j_s.d [%blink]
# CHECK-NEXT:  add %r0, %r0, %r1

--- |
  define i32 @f(i32 %a, i32 %b) { ret i32 0 }
...
---
name:            f
tracksRegLiveness: true
body:             |
  bb.0:
    liveins: $r0, $r1

    $r0 = ADD_rrr $r0, $r1
    J_S_BLINK implicit $blink
...
